Paper 1
Large Language Models for Software Engineering: A Systematic Literature Review (Hou et al., 2024)


This paper reviews how Large Language Models (LLMs), including ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot, are transforming software engineering. The authors analyse over one hundred research studies to understand the impact of LLMs across the software development lifecycle.

Key Findings
LLMs improve developer productivity by automating code generation.
They assist with debugging, code summarisation, documentation, and software testing.
AI tools reduce development time but still require human supervision.
Model hallucination and inaccurate code remain significant challenges.
Methodology
Systematic Literature Review (SLR)
Analysis of published software engineering studies
Comparative evaluation of AI applications
Contribution

This paper provides an up-to-date overview of AI applications in software engineering and identifies future research directions.

Paper 2
Security in Cloud-Native Services: A Survey (Psomas et al., 2023)
Overview

This survey discusses security issues in cloud-native environments that use containers, Kubernetes, and microservices. It explains modern cloud architectures and common cyber threats.

Key Findings
Cloud-native applications improve scalability and flexibility.
Containers introduce new security risks.
Kubernetes requires strong authentication and access control.
DevSecOps practices improve software security by integrating security into development.
Methodology
Survey of cloud security research
Comparative analysis of existing cloud-native security solutions
Review of container security frameworks
Contribution

The paper provides a strong theoretical foundation for cloud security and highlights best practices for securing modern cloud systems.

Paper 3
Vulnerabilities and Security Patches Detection in Open Source Software: A Survey (Lin et al., 2024)
Overview

This paper reviews techniques used to identify software vulnerabilities and security patches in open-source software. It focuses on machine learning, static analysis, and automated vulnerability detection.

Key Findings
Open-source software is widely used but vulnerable to security attacks.
Machine learning improves vulnerability detection accuracy.
Automated patch detection reduces response time.
Existing tools still produce false positives and false negatives.
Methodology
Systematic survey
Review of vulnerability detection techniques
Comparison of machine learning models
Contribution

The paper identifies current challenges in software security and suggests future directions for automated vulnerability detection.

Common Themes Across the Papers

Several common themes emerge from these three studies:

Artificial Intelligence is becoming increasingly important in software engineering.
Automation improves software development efficiency.
Security remains a major concern throughout the software lifecycle.
Machine learning is widely used for code analysis and vulnerability detection.
Human expertise is still essential because AI systems are not completely reliable.
Key Findings and Methodologies
Paper	Methodology	Major Findings
Hou et al. (2024)	Systematic Literature Review	LLMs improve coding, testing, and documentation but require human verification.
Psomas et al. (2023)	Survey	Cloud-native systems require stronger security mechanisms and DevSecOps practices.
